{'id': 'arc_opposite_edge_bar_marker_beams', 'kind': 'memory', 'title': 'Propagate marker beams between opposite edge bars in ARC grids', 'content': "For ARC grids with a uniform background, two long straight bars on opposite edges, and sparse marker cells of a third color, infer beam directions and segment-relative offset mapping from the geometry and demonstrations rather than assuming a fixed orientation. Keep edge-bar cells unchanged. In validated examples, sparse markers are recolored to 4, while each marker sends a straight beam in its original color through the background toward the aligned edge bar; fill only the cells between marker and bar, preserving cells on the other side unless the demonstrated projection rule selects them. Markers can also select parallel rows or columns on the opposite bar: measure each marker's offset along its source bar segment, copy that offset to the opposite bar's segment, then extend a ray inward from that segment. This can produce strokes in a disjoint row/column segment, including across the grid to the far edge where no bar is present. Extend projected rays all the way to the grid boundary, including the boundary cell when it is not itself part of a bar; preserve only actual bar cells. Validate both endpoints explicitly so no background gap remains at an unoccupied opposite edge. Validated variant: on an 11x7 grid with background 5, color-0 left bar at column 0 rows 4–6, color-0 right bar at column 6 rows 0–2, and color-7 markers at (4,3) and (5,3), recolor markers to 4; fill color 7 at columns 1–2 of rows 4–5 toward the left bar; marker offsets 0 and 1 within the left segment select rows 0 and 1 within the right segment, where color 7 fills columns 0–5, preserving the right-bar cells at column 6. Other validated examples establish the same principle for vertical side bars and marker offsets. Another validated case: on a 22x18 grid with background 9, color-0 bars at row 0 columns 3–4 and row 21 columns 16–17, and a color-1 marker at (5,3), recolor the marker to 4; fill color 1 at (1–4,3) toward the top bar, and map offset 0 from the top segment to column 16 of the bottom segment, extending the projected ray through to row 0 column 16 because that cell is not part of a bar. Confirm orientation, source segment, offset mapping, beam direction, and exact fill extent from examples; do not force this rule onto differently arranged bars without evidence.", 'path': 'arc', 'scope': 'global', 'reference': {}, 'arguments': {}, 'metadata': {'scope': 'global', 'evidence': 'Validated by correct submission on task-24b5104b; a prior incorrect submission omitted the far-edge cell at row 0, column 16.'}, 'source': 'refine', 'created_at': '2026-09-29T12:09:02.669400+00:00', 'updated_at': '2026-09-29T12:20:18.175Z', 'version': 3}
